00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:09,370
The question was simple:

6
00:00:09,370 --> 00:00:11,030
who killed Laura Palmer.

7
00:00:12,259 --> 00:00:13,625
The show was Twin Peaks,

8
00:00:13,625 --> 00:00:14,786
and for a while, that

9
00:00:14,786 --> 00:00:16,630
single unanswered question was

10
00:00:16,630 --> 00:00:17,496
strong enough to make

11
00:00:17,496 --> 00:00:18,650
people rearrange their week

12
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:19,900
around a television schedule.

13
00:00:21,369 --> 00:00:22,799
The question this video asks

14
00:00:22,799 --> 00:00:23,991
is not which dramas from

15
00:00:23,991 --> 00:00:26,140
the nineties people remember fondly.

16
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:28,637
Nostalgia explains why people

17
00:00:28,637 --> 00:00:29,490
rewatch a show.

18
00:00:30,109 --> 00:00:31,459
It does not explain why

19
00:00:31,459 --> 00:00:32,809
people showed up for it

20
00:00:32,809 --> 00:00:33,946
in the first place,

21
00:00:33,946 --> 00:00:35,173
week after week, back

22
00:00:35,173 --> 00:00:36,521
before streaming made a

23
00:00:36,521 --> 00:00:38,609
fixed schedule almost meaningless.

24
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:41,152
The real question is what

25
00:00:41,152 --> 00:00:43,019
these shows were actually doing,

26
00:00:43,019 --> 00:00:45,545
mechanically, that turned watching television

27
00:00:45,545 --> 00:00:46,872
into an appointment instead

28
00:00:46,872 --> 00:00:48,089
of a background activity.

29
00:00:49,489 --> 00:00:51,365
Three answers, in order: how

30
00:00:51,365 --> 00:00:53,404
they used ensembles, how they

31
00:00:53,404 --> 00:00:55,476
used cliffhangers, and how

32
00:00:55,476 --> 00:00:56,749
they earned their stakes.

33
00:00:57,919 --> 00:00:59,248
And one of those answers

34
00:00:59,248 --> 00:01:00,844
comes with a warning, because

35
00:01:00,844 --> 00:01:02,539
the same network that helped

36
00:01:02,539 --> 00:01:04,453
perfect the technique also

37
00:01:04,453 --> 00:01:05,785
broke it, on purpose,

38
00:01:05,785 --> 00:01:07,449
in someone else's show.

39
00:01:08,309 --> 00:01:09,809
Start with the ensemble.

40
00:01:10,349 --> 00:01:12,135
When E R premiered on

41
00:01:12,135 --> 00:01:13,607
N B C in September

42
00:01:13,607 --> 00:01:14,552
of nineteen ninety four, it

43
00:01:14,552 --> 00:01:16,339
did not center on one

44
00:01:16,339 --> 00:01:17,441
lead the way most

45
00:01:17,441 --> 00:01:18,650
dramas before it had.

46
00:01:19,689 --> 00:01:21,407
It followed an emergency room

47
00:01:21,407 --> 00:01:22,987
full of doctors and nurses,

48
00:01:22,987 --> 00:01:25,419
cutting between overlapping crises in

49
00:01:25,419 --> 00:01:27,212
something close to real time,

50
00:01:27,212 --> 00:01:28,801
so that a quiet character

51
00:01:28,801 --> 00:01:30,200
beat in one hallway sat

52
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:32,040
right next to a life-or-death

53
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,020
decision in the next room.

54
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:37,314
The two-hour pilot alone introduced

55
00:01:37,314 --> 00:01:39,331
a full roster: Doctor Mark

56
00:01:39,331 --> 00:01:41,917
Greene, Doctor Doug Ross, Doctor

57
00:01:41,917 --> 00:01:44,343
Susan Lewis, Doctor John Carter,

58
00:01:44,343 --> 00:01:46,364
and Nurse Carol Hathaway, none

59
00:01:46,364 --> 00:01:47,908
of them positioned as more

60
00:01:47,908 --> 00:01:49,058
important than the others.

61
00:01:50,418 --> 00:01:51,791
The show became the top-rated

62
00:01:51,791 --> 00:01:53,658
program on American television through

63
00:01:53,658 --> 00:01:54,837
the mid to late nineties,

64
00:01:54,837 --> 00:01:57,152
regularly pulling more than thirty

65
00:01:57,152 --> 00:01:59,143
million viewers, and a rotating

66
00:01:59,143 --> 00:02:00,718
ensemble was central to why.

67
00:02:01,718 --> 00:02:02,996
No single actor's schedule or

68
00:02:02,996 --> 00:02:04,121
storyline could stall the

69
00:02:04,121 --> 00:02:05,558
whole machine, because the

70
00:02:05,558 --> 00:02:06,738
show was never built

71
00:02:06,738 --> 00:02:08,127
around just one person.

72
00:02:09,167 --> 00:02:10,819
When one doctor's arc slowed

73
00:02:10,819 --> 00:02:12,678
down, three others were already

74
00:02:12,678 --> 00:02:14,494
mid-crisis, and the show's fast,

75
00:02:14,494 --> 00:02:16,255
overlapping camera style made that

76
00:02:16,255 --> 00:02:17,807
constant motion feel less like

77
00:02:17,807 --> 00:02:19,167
a writing choice and more

78
00:02:19,167 --> 00:02:20,398
like the actual pace of

79
00:02:20,398 --> 00:02:21,628
a real emergency room.

80
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:23,417
The audience was never

81
00:02:23,417 --> 00:02:24,467
waiting on one character

82
00:02:24,467 --> 00:02:25,567
to get interesting again.

83
00:02:26,407 --> 00:02:27,879
That structure did something else,

84
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:29,098
too, something easy to miss.

85
00:02:29,517 --> 00:02:30,550
It meant a viewer could

86
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:31,800
form a favorite without the

87
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:33,147
show depending on that favorite.

88
00:02:34,618 --> 00:02:36,514
Twenty different people could watch

89
00:02:36,514 --> 00:02:37,554
the same episode of E

90
00:02:37,554 --> 00:02:38,529
R and each walk away

91
00:02:38,529 --> 00:02:40,158
invested in a different doctor,

92
00:02:40,158 --> 00:02:41,786
which multiplied the reasons to

93
00:02:41,786 --> 00:02:43,181
come back next week instead

94
00:02:43,181 --> 00:02:44,516
of concentrating all of it

95
00:02:44,516 --> 00:02:45,837
