96
00:02:45,837 --> 00:02:47,157
might eventually go stale.

97
00:02:47,547 --> 00:02:48,512
It also meant the show

98
00:02:48,512 --> 00:02:49,906
could survive a cast departure

99
00:02:49,906 --> 00:02:50,882
that would have sunk a

100
00:02:50,882 --> 00:02:52,802
single-lead drama outright, since a

101
00:02:52,802 --> 00:02:54,787
rotating ensemble was designed from

102
00:02:54,788 --> 00:02:56,713
the beginning to absorb exactly

103
00:02:56,713 --> 00:02:58,353
that kind of change without

104
00:02:58,353 --> 00:02:59,618
the audience losing its

105
00:02:59,618 --> 00:03:00,737
reason to keep watching.

106
00:03:02,097 --> 00:03:03,875
Now, the cliffhanger, and the

107
00:03:03,875 --> 00:03:05,267
show that did it best.

108
00:03:06,638 --> 00:03:08,964
The X Files, which premiered

109
00:03:08,964 --> 00:03:10,638
on Fox in September of

110
00:03:10,638 --> 00:03:11,662
nineteen ninety three, ran two

111
00:03:11,662 --> 00:03:13,354
kinds of episodes side by

112
00:03:13,354 --> 00:03:16,114
side: freestanding stories often called

113
00:03:16,114 --> 00:03:18,246
monster of the week, built

114
00:03:18,246 --> 00:03:19,291
around F B I agents

115
00:03:19,291 --> 00:03:21,312
Mulder and Scully investigating a

116
00:03:21,312 --> 00:03:23,173
single strange case, and a

117
00:03:23,173 --> 00:03:24,966
connected mythology arc about a

118
00:03:24,966 --> 00:03:26,958
government conspiracy that the two

119
00:03:26,958 --> 00:03:28,186
of them kept almost,

120
00:03:28,186 --> 00:03:30,177
but never fully, uncovering.

121
00:03:31,297 --> 00:03:33,208
The mythology episodes were rationed

122
00:03:33,208 --> 00:03:35,357
deliberately rather than run constantly,

123
00:03:35,877 --> 00:03:37,092
and the show became known

124
00:03:37,092 --> 00:03:38,481
for ending entire seasons on

125
00:03:38,481 --> 00:03:40,951
some of the biggest cliffhangers

126
00:03:40,951 --> 00:03:43,252
television had produced, one widely

127
00:03:43,252 --> 00:03:45,066
cited example leaving both lead

128
00:03:45,066 --> 00:03:47,188
characters in mortal danger with

129
00:03:47,188 --> 00:03:48,940
no resolution offered until the

130
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:50,497
next season actually began.

131
00:03:51,387 --> 00:03:52,522
That rationing was the

132
00:03:52,522 --> 00:03:53,717
real mechanism at work.

133
00:03:54,187 --> 00:03:55,868
A cliffhanger only functions as

134
00:03:55,868 --> 00:03:57,549
appointment television if it is

135
00:03:57,549 --> 00:03:59,038
rare enough that the audience

136
00:03:59,038 --> 00:04:00,454
cannot predict when the next

137
00:04:00,454 --> 00:04:01,517
one is coming, so they

138
00:04:01,517 --> 00:04:02,990
simply have to keep watching

139
00:04:02,990 --> 00:04:04,279
every single week in case

140
00:04:04,279 --> 00:04:05,077
this is the one.

141
00:04:06,547 --> 00:04:08,123
Here is the warning.

142
00:04:08,123 --> 00:04:09,065
In nineteen ninety, A B

143
00:04:09,065 --> 00:04:11,178
C had arguably the biggest

144
00:04:11,178 --> 00:04:13,946
cliffhanger engine on television, and

145
00:04:13,946 --> 00:04:15,290
they broke it on purpose.

146
00:04:16,730 --> 00:04:17,876
Twin Peaks had built its

147
00:04:17,876 --> 00:04:19,365
entire first season around the

148
00:04:19,365 --> 00:04:20,767
open question of who killed

149
00:04:20,767 --> 00:04:22,693
Laura Palmer, and creator David

150
00:04:22,693 --> 00:04:24,120
Lynch never intended to

151
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:25,119
answer it at all.

152
00:04:25,909 --> 00:04:27,711
He wanted the mystery itself

153
00:04:27,711 --> 00:04:29,589
to be the engine, permanently,

154
00:04:30,039 --> 00:04:32,087
driving the show indefinitely without

155
00:04:32,087 --> 00:04:33,329
ever cashing itself in.

156
00:04:33,750 --> 00:04:36,192
Network executives disagreed, worried that

157
00:04:36,192 --> 00:04:38,003
an unanswered question could not

158
00:04:38,003 --> 00:04:39,497
sustain a series forever,

159
00:04:39,497 --> 00:04:40,992
and pressured the writers

160
00:04:40,992 --> 00:04:42,117
to reveal the killer

161
00:04:42,117 --> 00:04:43,629
partway through season two.

162
00:04:44,219 --> 00:04:45,764
The reveal aired in November

163
00:04:45,764 --> 00:04:46,472
of nineteen ninety, in the

164
00:04:46,472 --> 00:04:48,290
seventh episode of that season.

165
00:04:48,290 --> 00:04:50,325
Viewership began dropping

166
00:04:50,325 --> 00:04:52,714
almost immediately afterward.

167
00:04:53,183 --> 00:04:54,446
The mystery, it turned out,

168
00:04:54,446 --> 00:04:55,598
was not one ingredient in

169
00:04:55,598 --> 00:04:57,203
the show's appeal among several.

170
00:04:57,474 --> 00:04:58,383
It was load-bearing.

171
00:04:59,383 --> 00:05:01,003
Once it resolved, a meaningful

172
00:05:01,003 --> 00:05:02,498
share of the audience simply

173
00:05:02,498 --> 00:05:03,657
no longer had a reason

174
00:05:03,657 --> 00:05:04,401
to show up on a

175
00:05:04,401 --> 00:05:05,755
given week, and the show

176
00:05:05,755 --> 00:05:06,973
was cancelled not long

177
00:05:06,983 --> 00:05:08,193
after, on a cliffhanger

178
00:05:08,193 --> 00:05:09,530
nobody stuck around long

179
00:05:09,530 --> 00:05:10,803
enough to see resolved.

180
00:05:11,753 --> 00:05:12,903
That is the part modern

181
00:05:12,903 --> 00:05:14,478
television forgot in the other

182
00:05:14,478 --> 00:05:17,204
direction: the difference between a

183
00:05:17,204 --> 00:05:19,339
cliffhanger you ration carefully and

184
00:05:19,339 --> 00:05:20,410
a mystery you cash in

185
00:05:20,410 --> 00:05:22,142
too early because someone outside

186
00:05:22,142 --> 00:05:23,694
the writers room got nervous.

187
00:05:25,053 --> 00:05:27,112
Networks and streamers now regularly

188
00:05:27,112 --> 00:05:28,914
answer a show's central question

189
00:05:28,933 --> 00:05:30,723
inside its very first season,

190
00:05:30,723 --> 00:05:32,727
treating a serialized mystery as

191
00:05:32,727 --> 00:05:34,067
a retention risk if it

192
00:05:34,067 --> 00:05:35,784
runs too long unresolved.

193
00:05:37,173 --> 00:05:38,335
Twin Peaks is the proof

194
00:05:38,335 --> 00:05:39,925
that the opposite failure mode

195
00:05:39,925 --> 00:05:40,650
is just as real,

196
00:05:40,650 --> 00:05:42,123
and arguably more permanent.

197
00:05:43,243 --> 00:05:44,760
Answer the question too soon,

198
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:46,657
on someone else's schedule instead

199
00:05:46,657 --> 00:05:47,828
of the story's own, and

200
00:05:47,828 --> 00:05:48,935
you can kill the exact

201
00:05:48,935 --> 00:05:50,421
thing that was pulling people

202
00:05:50,421 --> 00:05:51,483
back in the first place.

203
00:05:52,563 --> 00:05:53,976
The third mechanic is the

204
00:05:53,976 --> 00:05:55,121
hardest one to fake:

205
00:05:55,121 --> 00:05:56,667
stakes that feel earned,

206
00:05:56,667 --> 00:05:58,143
not staged for shock.

207
00:05:59,143 --> 00:05:59,964
N Y P D Blue,

208
00:05:59,964 --> 00:06:02,154
created by Steven Bochco and

209
00:06:02,154 --> 00:06:03,979
David Milch and built in

210
00:06:03,979 --> 00:06:05,863
part from Milch's own experience

211
00:06:05,863 --> 00:06:07,508
with his father's death, spent

212
00:06:07,508 --> 00:06:10,036
years establishing Detective Bobby Simone

213
00:06:10,036 --> 00:06:11,343
as a steady, reliable

214
00:06:11,343 --> 00:06:12,577
presence on the show

215
00:06:12,577 --> 00:06:13,948
before ever writing him

216
00:06:13,948 --> 00:06:15,283
toward a fatal illness.

217
00:06:15,853 --> 00:06:17,281
Actor Jimmy Smits had appeared

218
00:06:17,281 --> 00:06:18,654
in roughly ninety episodes of

219
00:06:18,654 --> 00:06:19,693
the series by the time

220
00:06:19,693 --> 00:06:21,447
his character's death aired, in

221
00:06:21,447 --> 00:06:22,999
the fifth episode of season

222
00:06:22,999 --> 00:06:24,127
six, and the show let

223
00:06:24,127 --> 00:06:25,478
